faq(['Yes. A text can develop distinct but connected ideas, such as how people organize work and how they evaluate results. Their number must be established from the text, not assumed from its length.','Evidence is information offered as support. Opinions are judgments or beliefs and can also be claims. A ranking statistic can support a popularity claim, but does not by itself settle artistic quality.','A source and location let another reader check the wording, context and reasoning. Finding a line does not by itself establish that your interpretation is sound.','Look for claims, supporting information and missing context. Some persuasive writing is fair; evaluate specific wording and sources rather than assuming every writer is hiding facts.','Infer it from details and relationships, then explain the evidence. Your interpretation should remain open to revision as you read further.','Use quotation marks for exact borrowed words and cite the source. A faithful paraphrase also needs citation. Add your explanation of relevance; follow any instruction requiring direct quotations.']);
