faq(['Not every text serves the same purpose. When interpreting a narrative, identify an idea supported by its details instead of assuming every text carries one clear moral.','Readers interpret within the evidence of the work. An author’s comments can add context, but a claim about intention needs its own support. Different readings may fit, and some readings conflict with the text.','Present the evidence, explain the development and consider objections respectfully. For this task use three moments when the text provides them, rather than relying on whether a reading is expected.','No. Setting, minor characters, dialogue, structure and repeated details can all contribute. Explain how they work in this particular text.','Theme analysis practices inference and evidence-based interpretation. Other reading skills also transfer beyond literature; no single skill does all that work.','This task asks for one clear sentence. Length and punctuation alone do not determine quality. Make the idea precise enough to support from the text.','A text can introduce, complicate or qualify ideas as it develops. Your interpretation may change or be strengthened as you encounter more evidence; it need not become more skeptical simply because you reached the end.']);
