faq(['A zero price does not mean zero opportunity cost. Attending a free event uses time that might have another valued use; specify the relevant alternative rather than assuming every free object is scarce in the same way.','No. If the alternatives are equally valued, the forgone value can still be substantial even though you are indifferent between them. Opportunity cost is not the difference between their values or a measure of regret.','No. Time and space choices also involve trade-offs. Paying for a faster option may be reasonable under a person’s circumstances, but the price alone does not prove a choice is best.','Past investments can make people reluctant to stop. Separate unrecoverable past expenditure from remaining costs, benefits, information and recoverable value.','No. Scarcity is the general constraint of limited resources relative to wants. Poverty concerns inadequate resources for meeting important needs; its consequences are not the same as every ordinary trade-off.','Additional production can expand available options, but it uses resources. Examine which resources have alternative uses and their forgone value rather than assuming every worker or material is already assigned to another project.']);
